A f2-095 WRONG {'text': "The **load factor** is a term used in various fields, including **computer science**, **power systems**, and **data structures**. It is generally a measure of **how full** or **how utilized** a system or structure is at a given time.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **In Computer Science / Data Structures**:\nIn the context of **hash tables**, the **load factor** is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Load Factor} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Elements Stored}}{\\text{Total Number of Slots (or Buckets)}}\n$$\n\n- It indicates how full the hash table is.\n- A higher load factor means the hash table is more full, which may lead to more collisions.\n- Load factor is used to determine when to **resize** or **rehash** the hash table to maintain performance.\n- A common load factor threshold is **0.7** or **0.75**. When the load factor exceeds this, the hash table is usually resized (e.g., doubled in size).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **In Power Systems**:\nIn **electric power systems**, the **load factor** is defined as:\n\n$$\n\\text{Load Factor} = \\frac{\\text{Average Load}}{\\text{Peak Load}}\n$$\n\n- It measures how efficiently the electrical load is being used over a period of time.\n- A high load factor indicates that the load is more evenly distributed and that the system is being used efficiently.\n- A low load factor means the load is highly variable, and the system is underutilized at times.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **In Transportation / Logistics**:\nIn **transportation and logistics**, load factor refers to the **ratio of the actual load (weight or volume) to the total capacity of a vehicle or container**.\n\n$$\n\\text{Load Factor} = \\frac{\\text{Actual Load}}{\\text{Maximum Capacity}}\n$$\n\n- It indicates how full a vehicle or container is.\n- A high load factor means the vehicle is being used efficiently.\n- It is often used in the shipping and freight industry to optimize transportation costs and efficiency.\n\n---\n\n### Summary:\n| Field             | Definition                                                                 |\n|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Data Structures   | Number of elements / total slots (used in hash tables)                   |\n| Power Systems     | Average load / peak load                                                  |\n| Transportation    | Actual load / maximum capacity                                            |\n\nLet me know if you'd like an example in any of these contexts!"}
